{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1563171-v6-alpha-initial-playtest-report","id":1563171,"title":"V6 alpha initial playtest report","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-08-17T07:25:12-07:00","id":1563171,"title":"V6 alpha initial playtest report","content":"We played our first playtest last night. It took some getting used to the new systems, and there were a couple of things we didn't get to try, but it was overall fine and we had fun. Taking into account that this is an alpha playtest and so probably has lots of early ideas (likely way more than they'll use), these are my initial impressions:<br /> <br /> <b>Core mechanics</b><ul><li>TN 8+ was fine.</li> <li>Concerns about odds of success seem overblown. The players didn't seem to succeed or fail too often. Each player had at least one painful failure, which is a product of the much smaller dice pools.</li> <li>I initially wanted 10s to explode like VTR2e, since it gives neater probabilities (about a third of a success per die, instead of just 0.3), but since you only need one success to succeed and extra successes rarely do anything else, Quickening essentially means you're carrying 10s forward as bonus dice on later tests. It makes sense.</li> <li>Quickening is basically just Willpower that keys off success.</li> <li>Quickening does make it more exciting when you roll 10s, but I wouldn't say it's as exciting as rolling a natural 20, since it only gives a very minor buff (basically, +1 die per point). If it were me, I'd probably award it less frequently and make it more powerful, or at least allow it to be used more dynamically (e.g., to do tricks/gambits/feats in combat). Either way, it's not objectionable.</li> <li>The default roll always requires a negative modifier of some kind (usually \u20132 to \u20133 dice from Difficulty), which suggests they might need to recalibrate their baseline. Average Difficulty (or Challenging) feels like it should be +/-0 dice; Routine (or Average) should not be a penalty; it should be +1 die, minimum, so they should scale it upwards from there. AFAICS, there's no unopposed roll where you don't deduct dice at all, which just feels wrong. Why not just make all Attributes one dot lower in the first place?</li> <li>Applying dice pool penalties to every roll also feeds into the feeling that dice pools are significantly smaller. People wanted their fistfuls of dice.</li> <li>There's possibly not enough wiggle room in the system. At zero dice, you automatically fail but at four dice you can automatically succeed at anything outside of conflict. The range between &quot;totally impossible&quot; and &quot;so trivial I don't have to roll&quot; is too small.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Dice pools and modifiers</b><br /> <br /> It takes too long now to make a dice pool. This is due to a few things:<ul><li>There are too many modifiers in general (circumstantial Difficulties, opponent-based Difficulties, action-based Difficulties, and hunting modifiers). They should condense the four pages of Difficulties and modifiers into a single table.</li> <li>The order of operations for building a dice pool seems janky. Difficulty should be expressed as a modifier (\u20135 rather than 5), not least because the system combines Difficulty modifiers with separate dice pool penalties. There's an extra step involved when you have to first add Difficulty modifiers, then invert them to a negative, and then add the dice pool modifiers on top. If something is Difficulty 2 with a +1 Difficulty modifier, you have to remind yourself that a further \u20131 penalty actually brings that to \u20134 (-[2+1]-1) and not \u20132 (-[2+1-1]). Small things like this add up at the table.</li> <li>Besides the time required to build dice pools, players felt the actual resolution was quicker once they rolled, since they didn't need to compare outcomes. But since I can roll at the same time as them, it wasn't really quicker for me, as I had to watch them building their pools every time and that took quite some time.</li> <li>As an ST, I was busy enough not to miss rolling dice, but there's definitely more for players to track now.</li> </ul><br /> <b>NPCs and opposition</b><ul><li>NPCs felt pretty flat. One number for everything was dull. I'm hoping that's just an abstraction for the playtest and that they'll at least get different ratings for physical/mental/social. NPC Level should be an optional rule in any case.</li> <li>Since they do flat damage, fights become very predictable. They either do no damage or their Level in damage. The only risk is if the players roll a painful failure and opt to take additional damage. PvP combat (opposed tests) is more swingy and would probably feel better as the default system.</li> <li>I spent Drama regularly, but had three points by the end, so the game escalated nicely at that point. Because of how things panned out, they encountered three major obstacles at the end, instead of spread over the course of the session, and were very clever about managing them. I think that made it feel like they'd earned their getaway.</li> <li>There is an issue in that you don't want to have teleporting NPCs who just arrive from thin air. I tried to make the arrivals logical, which is why the police, third wight, and hounds all essentially ended up arriving at the same time.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Vitae</b><ul><li>We didn't mind the merging of health and the blood pool conceptually, but it does put a lot of pressure on that one health track. Making more Disciplines key off Willpower was helpful there, but it does lead to some weird interactions.</li> <li>For example, if you injure another vampire before diablerising them, you'll already find they've got less vitae in them. Meanwhile, mortals have to have a separate pool of 10 blood points that isn't affected by injury, because otherwise you'd be sacrificing blood every time you hurt a vessel.</li> <li>Fluff-wise, there are some issues with suspension of disbelief as well. In prior editions, vitae was spent to heal wounds, so you might end up with an empty blood pool but you're fully healed. It takes a lot to beat the vitae out of a torpid vampire. But in the alpha, any injury removes vitae, so it's almost like you're spending vitae to automatically heal the damage, but the damage stays anyway. One of my players joked that it felt like he was just gushing blood any time someone hurt him, like that scene in <i>The Addams Family</i>.</li> <li>I'm almost certain they'll end up splitting the health and vitae track again, but perhaps with the vitae track having the equivalent of health levels for Hunger. If they don't, that'll probably be my first house rule.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Morality</b><ul><li>The Morality system is a bit poorly worded in places, and is probably more complicated than it needs to be. They also introduce a lot of terms that perhaps add to the complexity (e.g., Beast/Monstrous, Nature/Mortal, &quot;sliding&quot;, &quot;re-opening&quot;, etc.). Whatever happened to the good old &quot;permanent&quot; dots and &quot;temporary&quot; points/squares?</li> <li>The players accumulated several marks in their Humanity tracker, but I fear they'll rarely trigger frenzy (outbursts seem silly, so we weren't bothered to miss those).</li> <li>The name Mortal Acts seemed misleading to us. It felt like that should describe something positive you do to challenge your Monstrous side, rather than merely describing harm to your Mortal side. &quot;Mortal Sins&quot; would be an obvious alternative.</li> <li>Also, I think there should be some asymmetry to the struggle against the Beast\u2014it should be easy to just let your Nature fade away, while the Beast never sits quietly.</li> <li>You mark damage to your track if you successfully diablerise someone, but not if you merely attempt to diablerise them and fail.</li> <li>The fact you only make one mark for murder seems way too low.</li> </ul><b>Disciplines and powers</b><ul><li>V6 has the same problems as V5 in the Disciplines department (super niche powers that don't really do much on their own, giving the illusion of choice by breaking old powers apart). However, maturing Disciplines are a great idea.</li> <li>We didn't get to use Blood Surge, so we will try that next time.</li> <li>It seemed there were fewer options to just have a Discipline work against mortals, so we'd like some of those to come back in future.</li> <li>Replacing that messy Blood Potency table with a Generation Modifier is a great idea. Just don't call it a Generation Modifier\u2014it can still be called Blood Potency.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Playtest and pregens</b><ul><li>The adventure is a bit artificial and bit more heist-like than we'd usually play, but I expect that's to allow you to try out a broad range of systems\u2014including investigation, infiltration, physical combat and social combat.</li> <li>The pregens seem pretty poorly put together. E.g., a character with a specialisation in light weapons only has a heavy weapon. With slightly more optimised characters, it may feel easier.</li> </ul><br /> All in all, we had fun and I think it's got good bones. It's just the add-ons that need work and refinement. I'm cautiously optimistic about the next draft.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":".  Edit: Formatting 08-17-2026, 10:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-17T09:14:11-07:00","id":1563174,"title":"","content":"Amazing! Thanks for doing this. I\u2019m looking forward to V6 when it finally comes out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-17T13:24:02-07:00","id":1563193,"title":"","content":"Interesting read as I will not be trying the playtest with my group so it's nice to see different takes and compare how people feel about what's in the playtest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2357-sathriel","name":"sathriel","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"121"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-18T03:14:37-07:00","id":1563217,"title":"","content":"The TN 8 I expected to work out, Quickening working out was interesting to know. I do feel it kinda weird that you can spend it in the actions of others, but my major complaint is just the name taken from Highlander (and just because it's too copy pastey, Diablerie has always been a pretty Highlander mechanic).<br /> <br /> I think your point on the default Difficulties lands well, I thought the same. Even if mostly it works, always having a penalty feels punitive. It also makes that Attribute at 1 be a sentence of futility.<br /> <br /> Let me do some calc. Your effective range of challenge is 1 to 3 dice. What kind of character should fall in that range for a given task, and who should fall out of it for the same task?<br /> <br /> By the Mediocrity Principle the bulk of our experience is average tasks being performed by average professionals even if it doesn't look like it, so we can assume that most services we see in our daily lives are being performed by people with around 4 dice including tools and environment. Why 4 dice? That's Attribute 2 + Skill 1 + Specialization, which seems a good rule of thumb for a trained but not great worker. Since most services can be expected to be performed fine without issue, having 4+ dice (auto success) after Diff modifier should be ok, -1 shouldn't.<br /> <br /> This is not the easiest roll. There must be a point where a trainee can feel comfortable with tasks that aren't trivial for others. Yes, I think that starting at +1 Diff and progress from there to negatives is spot on if 4 = auto success. Is that the best option?<br /> <br /> We have to tackle this from three perspectives:<br /> 1. Are the odds high enough that rolling is more a chore than worth it?<br /> 2. Is the character's competence high enough that failure becomes a narrative problem?<br /> 3. Is the difference between characters high enough to justify a given task challenging one but not the other?<br /> <br /> 1. The chances of success by number of dice on the pool, calculated at AnyDice:<br /> 1 dice 30% worth a try, but unlikely<br /> 2 dice 51% pure luck, unreliable<br /> 3 dice 65,7% likely success, but a 34,3% failure<br /> 4 dice 75,99% only 24,1% failure, roughly 1/4<br /> 5 dice 83,19% only 16,81% failure, roughly 1/6<br /> 6 dice 88,24% only 11,76% failure, less than 1/8<br /> <br /> I can see a case for either 4 or 5 as the point of auto success in this perspective. 24,1% is lower than the minimum chance of success, but is still one failure every 4 rolls. Rolling 5 dice definitely is unnecessary, but 4 I'm not inclined one way or another.<br /> <br /> 2 and 3. What having a pool above Difficulty penalty entails?<br /> -- 4 dice above is having 3 more than the minimum necessary (From an unskilled child to a working professional. From an average trainee to a strong veteran with Att 3 and Skill 3. From this Veteran to a master at Olympic level, just short of peak human performance), 2 more than dumb luck (+1 rank in both Attribute and Skill) and 1 more than a 2/3 chance of success (you had a solid chance, then bought a new rank).<br /> -- 5 dice above is having 4 more than the minimum necessary (From an unskilled child to a talented professional. From an average trainee to an exceptional veteran with Att least one trait at 4. From strong Veteran to absolute peak human performance), 3 more than dumb luck, 2 more than a 2/3 chance of success (you had a solid chance, then bought two new ranks) and 1 more than a 3/4 chance (already diminished returns to only now buy auto success).<br /> <br /> 5 dice seems a bit excessive for me here. Even if you need to be a strong Veteran to have a chance, should you really have to be at a most absolute peak human possibility to be able to auto success it? I think 4 dice is ok and values more the dots you buy. Spending XP to go from 2/3 chance to 3/4 chance isn't really that fun, the auto success gives qualitative difference when numeric would be too little. Both the difference between chars and the numeric return seem to me that auto success at a 4 is a worthy time saver for unopposed tasks and a nice boost to the value of your XP.<br /> <br /> I think the rule is solid. Wouldn't find 5 terrible, but 4 is solid.<br /> <br /> How did the modifiers played? I didn't got it, the four tables, three are for completely different types of roll, so choosing which to use and the Diff within should be up to you and simple, only one is really circumstantial modifiers.<br /> <br /> I just mean by that I'm not picturing how it played to understand what slowed things down. I do think the Diff modifiers could have a better explanation, and the main table have some examples.<br /> <br /> Players having more to track is fine in my book. Less strain on the ST and more participation by the players in building the story. Overall I like it, though I can see it faltering with some types of players that can't or won't make things easy.<br /> <br /> The flat NPCs was a problem I expected. I usually prefer about 6 or 7 stats, 3 baselines for unexpected situations and 3 or 4 actions the NPC is good at. That's my minimum even for random mooks.<br /> <br /> But the Drama points system, I'm really curious about how it played out in practice. How was it to use? What it changed and what went harder or smoother thanks to it? How you'd say it fared over unguided improvisation and/or pre customization of the adventure?<br /> <br /> I personally think the Vitae track will work all right if fluff changes to the body healing minor injuries all the time or a clearer explanation that Vitae is a supernatural animation energy, not literal blood, and how it should look when taking damage. Overall I think the suspension of disbelief/blood shower comedy issue is the easy part to fix.<br /> <br /> How the pressure on a single track felt is more of an experience I'm curious about, and you have good points that &quot;bleeding&quot; a target by the beating is a significant change to how conflicts and predation among vampires play out. Split blood and HP for the living is a no brainer.<br /> <br /> Humanity I expected to play very different, and I don't think the point is to change balance, but first to feel if the proposed thematic change is interesting to begin with.<br /> <br /> It's not about falling to the Beast anymore, but about two conflicting inner sets of instinct and psychological pressure. None should be good or bad, balancing should be the challenge, it is not at all about the alien corruption eating your morality, but you balancing human yearnings and predator instinct, both for good and evil, a Forsaken Harmony type of thing.<br /> <br /> That's my curiosity in this regard. Not if their Scale works for the classic drama, but if the new conflict is an interesting play experience on its own terms.<br /> <br /> As a descriptive element it isn't new. It has been baked into the description of how kindred live in the conflict between human longing and social needs vs the Beast's demands for safety and paranoia, and especially how to roleplay a given Humanity value in the regular track. It's changing which aspect is the core conflict and which takes place as personal stylization.<br /> <br /> So, I'd really appreciate an evaluation of the proposal for a new experience itself as its own thing.<br /> <br /> Generation Modifier feels ok as a name for me. The point was precisely to ditch Blood Potency as something that changes with time within your Generation, going back to Generation being the thing Masquerade is about.<br /> <br /> Observe that when it talks about char gen and Tiers, it does have an observation that a young vampire from a lower Gen will use the Gen as basis for which Tier to use. It's poorly worded, but is a backpedaling on the Blood Potency trait and instead a simpler approach for the old Generation chart.<br /> <br /> I think the pregens are also built kind whatever because focus was to test char gen. I'd like to see some experience with it. Overall that was a good read for practical perspective, thank you.<br /> <br /> Hope you don't mind some questions and requests (that you're obviously not required to fulfill, your own experiments should be your main concern):<br /> <br /> - Choose Your Pain, how it played out? Fun to use? Fast or slow to decide? Are the options all interesting, or something proved evidently best of useless in practice?<br /> <br /> - Devil's Bargain? V5 has a heavy take on Diff partly because Win at a Cost is meant to be a core mechanic there, and I feel it actually work well. How does Devil's Bargain works as basic catch up for the steep penalties?<br /> <br /> - The system is not meant to use Difficulty only as a means to move you in and out of rolling range, but also to give you reason to engage with Side Resources, of which we have five types here. How did they play out as things to try and experience?<br /> -- Quickening economy,<br /> -- Assist, Demoralize, Distract and Find Flaw as action options that grant direct bonuses,<br /> -- Coordinate as a new Action on top of those but with its own bonus economy system,<br /> -- Modifiers as strategic approach to problems,<br /> -- Story Assets, the new simplified rule for equipment.<br /> <br /> - Vitae Thresholds are easy to track? Are they interesting in practice? Did the players just avoid going low, or the experience of playing at each range was fun or strategically engaging?<br /> <br /> - Baneful Damage eliminated damage type rollout and can now be downgraded during the night rather than in Daysleep. How it played out in your test? I like it in theory, want to know in practice.<br /> <br /> - Downtime rules are interesting over a more freeform take? Especially their new takes on Resources that interact with it, like spending and recovery, and reallocation, how they're going?<br /> <br /> - V5 had the concept of Aggravated Willpower damage that I didn't tested yet in the game I'm playing. The Playtest ditched it. How does that feel? Would a rule for Baneful Willpower damage improve this aspect of play?<br /> <br /> - Are Called Shots working well? And Social Conflict, is it easy to decide on rolls and dynamic to play? Conditions work well, and is the current list sufficient? <hr /><br /> I do have some ideas of rule changes that I'd like to see. Not just for this playtest but in general. New takes on several traditional approaches in systems.<br /> <br /> For one, I think it way past the time for we to get out of micromanaged seconds long turns. I think even combat turns should cover 1 to 5 minutes and represent an abstraction of what transpired after a &quot;<a href=\"https://sfconservatoryofdance.org/choreography-glossarsy/dance-phrase/\" target=\"_blank\">phrase</a>&quot; rather than a frame by frame slice that ends up requiring every second to be relevant.<br /> <br /> (Disclaimer: the link talks about dance, but choreography phrases are also used in fight scenes in movies and theater as the basic unit of meaningful movement)<br /> <br /> Another is that for social contested rolls I really, really like L5R 4th Edition use of Etiquette as the Defense standard. It's interesting because it captures an important aspect of interaction most games miss: standing and social maneuvering.<br /> <br /> Basically L5R avoids having social actions prescribe roleplay. Be it a persuasion, telling a lie or anything of the like, while NPCs can have a Difficulty for a given task to see if you did or not a convincing argument, PCs aren't required to resist such rolls in their decision making and even the ST can forego for NPCs without issue.<br /> <br /> Instead, you roll when you want to <i>publicly act or argue</i> the other's action, not because of how they affect you, but to see how your reaction is seen by the community, especially in a court setting. Can you denounce a lie without loosing face? Deny a request while giving the right impression? Counter an argument without sounding aggressive?<br /> <br /> We're moving past the effect of the social action in the interlocutor and moving into who can sway the social environment in their favor with the exchange, and who'll have to choose between conceding or loosing face. It's fascinating and I felt as the best social conflict system I've played just with this use of skills and test results. A Reputation stat could help in this regard, but isn't required.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-23T13:16:47-07:00","id":1563371,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1563171-v6-alpha-initial-playtest-report?p=1563217#post1563217\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The TN 8 I expected to work out, Quickening working out was interesting to know. I do feel it kinda weird that you can spend it in the actions of others, but my major complaint is just the name taken from Highlander (and just because it's too copy pastey, Diablerie has always been a pretty Highlander mechanic).</div> </div> </div> </div>Incidentally, Quickening is an old name for blood buffing, from <i>Ghouls: Fatal Addiction</i>. I don't mind the name at all, but the fluff reasoning for it seems a little iffy. I personally prefer the name Quickening to Blood Surge, but only for that same phenomenon. The thing we have now isn't either of those; it's a Momentum or Advantage system, so should be called something more fitting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think your point on the default Difficulties lands well, I thought the same. Even if mostly it works, always having a penalty feels punitive. It also makes that Attribute at 1 be a sentence of futility.<br /> <br /> Let me do some calc. Your effective range of challenge is 1 to 3 dice. What kind of character should fall in that range for a given task, and who should fall out of it for the same task?<br /> <br /> By the Mediocrity Principle the bulk of our experience is average tasks being performed by average professionals even if it doesn't look like it, so we can assume that most services we see in our daily lives are being performed by people with around 4 dice including tools and environment. Why 4 dice? That's Attribute 2 + Skill 1 + Specialization, which seems a good rule of thumb for a trained but not great worker. Since most services can be expected to be performed fine without issue, having 4+ dice (auto success) after Diff modifier should be ok, -1 shouldn't.<br /> <br /> This is not the easiest roll. There must be a point where a trainee can feel comfortable with tasks that aren't trivial for others. Yes, I think that starting at +1 Diff and progress from there to negatives is spot on if 4 = auto success. Is that the best option?<br /> <br /> We have to tackle this from three perspectives:<br /> 1. Are the odds high enough that rolling is more a chore than worth it?<br /> 2. Is the character's competence high enough that failure becomes a narrative problem?<br /> 3. Is the difference between characters high enough to justify a given task challenging one but not the other?<br /> <br /> 1. The chances of success by number of dice on the pool, calculated at AnyDice:<br /> 1 dice 30% worth a try, but unlikely<br /> 2 dice 51% pure luck, unreliable<br /> 3 dice 65,7% likely success, but a 34,3% failure<br /> 4 dice 75,99% only 24,1% failure, roughly 1/4<br /> 5 dice 83,19% only 16,81% failure, roughly 1/6<br /> 6 dice 88,24% only 11,76% failure, less than 1/8<br /> <br /> I can see a case for either 4 or 5 as the point of auto success in this perspective. 24,1% is lower than the minimum chance of success, but is still one failure every 4 rolls. Rolling 5 dice definitely is unnecessary, but 4 I'm not inclined one way or another.<br /> <br /> 2 and 3. What having a pool above Difficulty penalty entails?<br /> -- 4 dice above is having 3 more than the minimum necessary (From an unskilled child to a working professional. From an average trainee to a strong veteran with Att 3 and Skill 3. From this Veteran to a master at Olympic level, just short of peak human performance), 2 more than dumb luck (+1 rank in both Attribute and Skill) and 1 more than a 2/3 chance of success (you had a solid chance, then bought a new rank).<br /> -- 5 dice above is having 4 more than the minimum necessary (From an unskilled child to a talented professional. From an average trainee to an exceptional veteran with Att least one trait at 4. From strong Veteran to absolute peak human performance), 3 more than dumb luck, 2 more than a 2/3 chance of success (you had a solid chance, then bought two new ranks) and 1 more than a 3/4 chance (already diminished returns to only now buy auto success).<br /> <br /> 5 dice seems a bit excessive for me here. Even if you need to be a strong Veteran to have a chance, should you really have to be at a most absolute peak human possibility to be able to auto success it? I think 4 dice is ok and values more the dots you buy. Spending XP to go from 2/3 chance to 3/4 chance isn't really that fun, the auto success gives qualitative difference when numeric would be too little. Both the difference between chars and the numeric return seem to me that auto success at a 4 is a worthy time saver for unopposed tasks and a nice boost to the value of your XP.<br /> <br /> I think the rule is solid. Wouldn't find 5 terrible, but 4 is solid. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I have no problem with auto success at 4 if the baseline is shifted. Deducting from every roll just slows things down and feels weird.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How did the modifiers played? I didn't got it, the four tables, three are for completely different types of roll, so choosing which to use and the Diff within should be up to you and simple, only one is really circumstantial modifiers. </div> </div> </div>Sure, but the three tables/page of hunting modifiers <i>seems</i> too much. If you had a single table, you could have a column for the modifier, a description of what that means and what level of opposition you face (Average/Standard or Moderate/Skilled), and then a description with brief examples that can include circumstantial and hunting mods. Something like this: <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"></tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>\u20131</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Routine / Weakling</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Easy if well equipped or well trained. The weakest possible opponents, structures or objects. Minor obstacles (recent rain when following tracks, being upwind of a target, light fog).</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>\u20132 to \u20133</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Average / Standard</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Something a competent individual would only rarely fail at. Standard opposition with some competence. Standard and everyday objects built with some durability or longevity. Hiding in plain sight, light cover. Heavily guarded target.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>\u20134 to \u20135</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Moderate / Skilled</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Challenging feats. More competent individuals with special training or gear. Neonates, well trained mortals. Well-hidden behind camouflage. Password-protected, in an ancient language or cipher, or similar.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>\u20136 to \u20137</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Hard / Strong</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Actions that a trained individual would fail more often than succeed. Strong opposition. Experienced neonates, ancillae vampires, master hunters, young werewolves, brick wall, basic concrete slab, old car door.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>\u20138 to \u20139</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Severe / Powerful</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Things that even well-trained people find difficult. Powerful opponents that can challenge a whole coterie by themselves. Experienced ancillae vampires, legendary hunters, steel-reinforced concrete, engineered wood, stone wall.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>\u201310</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Extreme / Mythical</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Actions that challenge even the most competent individuals in the field. More powerful and influential opponents than most can even dream about (experienced elders, methuselahs, werewolf champions). Obstacles such as diamond, titanium, tungsten, silicon carbide.</td> </tr> </table></div> You could then have the separate tables as well for more detail.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I just mean by that I'm not picturing how it played to understand what slowed things down. I do think the Diff modifiers could have a better explanation, and the main table have some examples. </div> </div> </div>Normally, people count out their dots in Attribute, then in a Skill, and rarely take some dice off for modifiers. But here, they have to add Attribute, Skill, Focus, and then Difficulty (on every roll). It just felt like a lot of bean counting.<br /> <br /> People may have been quicker if they'd calculated their pools in their heads and only then picked up dice, but then you lose the tactile aspect of the counting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the Drama points system, I'm really curious about how it played out in practice. How was it to use? What it changed and what went harder or smoother thanks to it? How you'd say it fared over unguided improvisation and/or pre customization of the adventure? </div> </div> </div>It was basically fine. It came at a steady rate and reminded me to keep escalating when the players effed up. When there's a lot going on and it's a new system, having Drama is a nice way to measure tension and a reminder to bring things into the game.<br /> <br /> My players are really clever, and almost got away without any real confrontation. But near the end, I had three Drama left, so it reminded me to bring in some things I'd been planning to bring in, and it worked out really well. These complications essentially converged all at once\u2014an extra wight, the hunters, and the hounds\u2014and the police had already been called and were on their way.<br /> <br /> With a good roll and some clever decisions, they evaded the hunters and the wight, but nearly drove into the hounds and the road got blocked by multiple police vehicles. The players were able to convince the police that the men with guns were the real threat, and the sound of firearms as the hunters found the wight sold the misdirect.<br /> <br /> I didn't need the Drama. I could've done all of this stuff anyway. But when you're juggling a lot already, it's good to have a physical reminder. It's also an excellent way to make sure you're scaling challenges accordingly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I personally think the Vitae track will work all right if fluff changes to the body healing minor injuries all the time or a clearer explanation that Vitae is a supernatural animation energy, not literal blood, and how it should look when taking damage. Overall I think the suspension of disbelief/blood shower comedy issue is the easy part to fix.<br /> <br /> How the pressure on a single track felt is more of an experience I'm curious about, and you have good points that &quot;bleeding&quot; a target by the beating is a significant change to how conflicts and predation among vampires play out. Split blood and HP for the living is a no brainer. </div> </div> </div>None of us hated the combined tracker. It just needs work, and I think it's going to be the most contentious part for a lot of people. If it's a choice between this and Hunger Dice? Give me this, every time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Humanity I expected to play very different, and I don't think the point is to change balance, but first to feel if the proposed thematic change is interesting to begin with.<br /> <br /> It's not about falling to the Beast anymore, but about two conflicting inner sets of instinct and psychological pressure. None should be good or bad, balancing should be the challenge, it is not at all about the alien corruption eating your morality, but you balancing human yearnings and predator instinct, both for good and evil, a Forsaken Harmony type of thing.<br /> <br /> That's my curiosity in this regard. Not if their Scale works for the classic drama, but if the new conflict is an interesting play experience on its own terms. </div> </div> </div>Frankly, it probably won't come up in a single session like this. You'll mark off a few checks, but there isn't enough in the playtest packet to trigger something more (at least, it didn't feel like there was). It would be interesting to explore this long-term.<br /> <br /> I'm really not sold on Outbursts\u2014they feel like needless melodrama\u2014but if they're willing to do more with the four different tracks I won't mind as much. There's a lot of tracking right now, but it doesn't really feel worth it for what you get in return.<br /> <br /> That said, we might feel differently after we've played it a few times and have had to time to interact with that part of the mechanics in more detail.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Generation Modifier feels ok as a name for me. The point was precisely to ditch Blood Potency as something that changes with time within your Generation, going back to Generation being the thing Masquerade is about. </div> </div> </div>And I agree with that change, but the name doesn't help with the (mostly overblown and unimaginative) comparisons to D&amp;D. Having the exact same thing but just calling it Blood Potency or something more evocative would be better. &quot;Generation Modifier&quot; just feels very mechanical.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Observe that when it talks about char gen and Tiers, it does have an observation that a young vampire from a lower Gen will use the Gen as basis for which Tier to use. It's poorly worded, but is a backpedaling on the Blood Potency trait and instead a simpler approach for the old Generation chart. </div> </div> </div>Yes, I got that. I've seen people claiming that age and Generation have somehow been combined, and I think that's just people missing the point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think the pregens are also built kind whatever because focus was to test char gen. I'd like to see some experience with it. </div> </div> </div>I'd like to do that, too. One of my players tried but didn't realise he could get a PDF until an hour before our game. He'd been driving himself mad with Nexus. Everyone else was too busy (kids, work, 7-day shifts, etc.).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Overall that was a good read for practical perspective, thank you.<br /> <br /> Hope you don't mind some questions and requests (that you're obviously not required to fulfill, your own experiments should be your main concern): </div> </div> </div>No problem at all. Happy it's been helpful and will answer as best as I can.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - Choose Your Pain, how it played out? Fun to use? Fast or slow to decide? Are the options all interesting, or something proved evidently best of useless in practice? </div> </div> </div>It was fine. They basically chose the same thing every time (lose all Quickening), but my husband gave me drama one time. I can't see people opting to take Vitae or Willpower damage much, if at all.<br /> <br /> I would probably prefer to pick the options for the player, and let them pick between two things: either an in-game consequence or a loss of my choice of resource. That would vary things a bit and feel more like classic World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - Devil's Bargain? V5 has a heavy take on Diff partly because Win at a Cost is meant to be a core mechanic there, and I feel it actually work well. How does Devil's Bargain works as basic catch up for the steep penalties? </div> </div> </div>Devil's Bargains are basically also fine. If you like Win at a Cost, it's basically the same thing, but more codified. I realise my responses are very similar to a lot of this, but that's my overall reaction to the system\u2014we had fun, and the system's basically fine, but there's nothing I really hate or really love. Which is fine, really, because all we wanna do is play, and this lets us do that. But it's also probably not going to be my favourite edition.<br /> <br /> Personally, I would probably combine Devil's Bargains and Choose Your Pain. It again cuts down on some of the many choices you have. Devil's Bargains would be where you get a complication but also succeed, and Choose Your Pain would be where it's a failure and a complication. De-emphasise the menu-like mechanical aspect and refocus things on the narrative aspect instead.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - The system is not meant to use Difficulty only as a means to move you in and out of rolling range, but also to give you reason to engage with Side Resources, of which we have five types here. How did they play out as things to try and experience? </div> </div> </div>It was definitely a lot to manage for a first game, but it would get easier as time goes on. I suspect that if you like to get lost in RP, it'll be more intrusive than you'd otherwise like, but it wasn't laborious. It's about as fiddly as something like classic <i>Kindred of the East</i>\u2014that only has three Chi types plus Willpower, but you'll probably spend points less often in V6.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> -- Quickening economy, </div> </div> </div>My players used it fairly often. They never got above three, I believe, but that's because they so often needed the extra dice. However, you probably wouldn't need Quickening if you didn't need to deduct Difficulty off every single roll. Then you could just go back to Willpower for nudging the odds of success.<br /> <br /> One nice thing is that it gives you a tiny bit of a buzz from rolling a 10. Not much, because it's only worth one die, but enough.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> -- Assist, Demoralize, Distract and Find Flaw as action options that grant direct bonuses, </div> </div> </div>We only used Assist of these, but I can't really remember much about it either way.<br /> <br /> [QUOTE]-- Coordinate as a new Action on top of those but with its own bonus economy system,<br /> -- Modifiers as strategic approach to problems,[/QUTE]<br /> <br /> We probably wouldn't use these that often anyway, and we didn't get much chance to do so this time. I have one player who probably would, but the others aren't really interested in playing that way, so they'd mostly be wasted, I think.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, if they turned Quickening into something like Group Advantage from WFRP4e, with a small but broad range of options, then they might. I could print a one-page summary for them and they'd probably use it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> -- Story Assets, the new simplified rule for equipment. </div> </div> </div>This all works well. This is how I prefer to use equipment and assets anyway. Again, though, most of it didn't come up much in the playtest.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - Vitae Thresholds are easy to track? Are they interesting in practice? Did the players just avoid going low, or the experience of playing at each range was fun or strategically engaging? </div> </div> </div>I like the approach in general. They didn't use/lose enough Vitae for it to matter much. But also, as this was a playtest, they all decided to try out diablerie on the wights, so they were able to top up pretty easily.<br /> <br /> I think it would help to have a visual reminder of the thresholds on the sheets. Something like the old health levels. This is exactly what I've wanted from a Hunger system, so I think it's a good approach, but they could maybe beef the differences up\u2014e.g., I'd give Requiem-style Kindred Senses at Thirsty, and maybe Lashing Out at Hungry. Something like that, to make Hunger feel gnarlier.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - Baneful Damage eliminated damage type rollout and can now be downgraded during the night rather than in Daysleep. How it played out in your test? I like it in theory, want to know in practice.<br /> - Downtime rules are interesting over a more freeform take? Especially their new takes on Resources that interact with it, like spending and recovery, and reallocation, how they're going? </div> </div> </div>I like these additions in theory, but we never got to try them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - V5 had the concept of Aggravated Willpower damage that I didn't tested yet in the game I'm playing. The Playtest ditched it. How does that feel? Would a rule for Baneful Willpower damage improve this aspect of play? </div> </div> </div>This is probably a good thing. Aggravated WP damage was too hard to recover in V5.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - Are Called Shots working well? And Social Conflict, is it easy to decide on rolls and dynamic to play? Conditions work well, and is the current list sufficient? </div> </div> </div>Called shots seemed pretty straightforward. We did use those quite a bit. Social combat we didn't use. Conditions seemed fine.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For one, I think it way past the time for we to get out of micromanaged seconds long turns. I think even combat turns should cover 1 to 5 minutes and represent an abstraction of what transpired after a &quot;<a href=\"https://sfconservatoryofdance.org/choreography-glossarsy/dance-phrase/\" target=\"_blank\">phrase</a>&quot; rather than a frame by frame slice that ends up requiring every second to be relevant.<br /> <br /> (Disclaimer: the link talks about dance, but choreography phrases are also used in fight scenes in movies and theater as the basic unit of meaningful movement) </div> </div> </div>This could work well. How would you do it?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Another is that for social contested rolls I really, really like L5R 4th Edition use of Etiquette as the Defense standard. It's interesting because it captures an important aspect of interaction most games miss: standing and social maneuvering.<br /> <br /> Basically L5R avoids having social actions prescribe roleplay. Be it a persuasion, telling a lie or anything of the like, while NPCs can have a Difficulty for a given task to see if you did or not a convincing argument, PCs aren't required to resist such rolls in their decision making and even the ST can forego for NPCs without issue.<br /> <br /> Instead, you roll when you want to <i>publicly act or argue</i> the other's action, not because of how they affect you, but to see how your reaction is seen by the community, especially in a court setting. Can you denounce a lie without loosing face? Deny a request while giving the right impression? Counter an argument without sounding aggressive?<br /> <br /> We're moving past the effect of the social action in the interlocutor and moving into who can sway the social environment in their favor with the exchange, and who'll have to choose between conceding or loosing face. It's fascinating and I felt as the best social conflict system I've played just with this use of skills and test results. A Reputation stat could help in this regard, but isn't required. </div> </div> </div>Nice. I'm gonna have to check that our.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":".  Edit: Typo 08-23-2026, 04:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-23T17:24:15-07:00","id":1563389,"title":"","content":"Very nice results, I think we have a solid start. And Drama is maybe more useful than I have it credit for.<br /> <br /> Nexus is horribly coded, idk what they're doing there that it's so heavy to run such a simple site. I did manage to make a char in it, though, which was when I found out you basically can't make a Ministry without at least 1 dot in Corruption because you'll be without enough prerequisites for your starting Clan Traits. But it was otherwise fine at least in the site.<br /> <br /> Last night I was playing V5 and did felt I could use something like Coordinate or Find Weakness. Se got into a fight and two PCs, one being mine, had zero attack options whatsoever. Those options are good in that they add something for characters you really don't want to give combat options to (mine is a Japanese dev, she really doesn't see a point in solving things by hitting things when you live in a society where you can let better prepared professionals do that if push comes to shove. I'd feel it more in-character for her to observe and give information rather forcing myself to learn combat traits just to have valid game actions).<br /> <br /> For Phrase based turns I'm still thinking what it could benefit from in terms of mechanical changes, but I think in most systems it's more of a narrative change than a profound change in mechanics.<br /> <br /> What may be worth adding is a new system for movement where instead of positioning at will, you roll something to define where in the field you end up at the end of the phrase/turn.<br /> <br /> Again I can take something from L5R. It has a skill called Battle, and one of its main uses is to move through mass battles. You roll it, and depending on the result you can say if you got to move somewhere you needed, find specific people or things in the midst of the skirmish, or what kinds of mini events or opportunities you got for yourself in there. With some fiddling it may become a system to find out if during the few minutes phrase you managed a tactical position, got hold of a target or ended corralled into a bad corner.<br /> <br /> L5R isn't the ultimate system or anything, but it does have a number of ideas I really liked and am thinking about transplanting to other games.<br /> <br /> As for V6, I think it may end up in a similar position to D&amp;D 5th E: it has very few rules that stand out in the history of the game, but the full thing just runs so fine that it's easy the best edition ever, simply because it's the one that'll less often get in your way. It works consistently and that's a greater advantage than it seems.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-31T16:40:11-07:00","id":1563718,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1563171-v6-alpha-initial-playtest-report?p=1563389#post1563389\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Very nice results, I think we have a solid start. And Drama is maybe more useful than I have it credit for.<br /> <br /> Nexus is horribly coded, idk what they're doing there that it's so heavy to run such a simple site. I did manage to make a char in it, though, which was when I found out you basically can't make a Ministry without at least 1 dot in Corruption because you'll be without enough prerequisites for your starting Clan Traits. But it was otherwise fine at least in the site.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well, many playtesters seem to disagree as Jess Lanzillo put Drama in the Miss category when writing about the early feedback from the playtest surveys. <br /> <br /> And was that a Nexus problem? I remember people complaining that RAW there are some Disciplines that you have to take because of the prerequisites.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2357-sathriel","name":"sathriel","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"121"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-31T17:33:51-07:00","id":1563725,"title":"","content":"The early feedback is the one place where I expect the less useful return. It has more people playing while still not blooded by whatever they didn't like at first and more people who filled the form without actually playing.<br /> <br /> But I don't know. I didn't tried it yet and I'm not here asking for a statistical survey, but the deep dive of a fellow poster. Not better or worse, but a qualitatively distinct type of data.<br /> <br /> And the Nexus problem was just how the site is, the need for Corruption part you're right, it was a legit prerequisite failure.<br /> <br /> Nexus is heavy and slow for no good reason. It has an ok layout, but nothing that justify just how much it seems to drain both band and processing power, so it is just painfully slow, but no actual major bugs that could mess your character sheet, no.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-01T04:40:17-07:00","id":1563754,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1563171-v6-alpha-initial-playtest-report?p=1563725#post1563725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And the Nexus problem was just how the site is, the need for Corruption part you're right, it was a legit prerequisite failure.<br /> <br /> Nexus is heavy and slow for no good reason. It has an ok layout, but nothing that justify just how much it seems to drain both band and processing power, so it is just painfully slow, but no actual major bugs that could mess your character sheet, no.</div> </div> </div> </div>Got ya, I grabbed the playtest off drivethrurpg cause I could not be bothered to register to another service just for that. Our group